  <h1 class="heading">A short word on generative AI models</h1>

  <p><strong>TLDR;</strong> Workshop.codes is a platform that fosters and encourages creators. AI models are build on the wonderful, silly, open, and hard work of all creators alike, with the goal of profiting off and pushing out those same creators. These models can not in good conscience be considered helpful or part of the greater good, as their supposed help is a doubled-edged sword. Workshop.codes wishes to nourish creation, not replace it.</p>

  <hr>

  <p>The internet is a wonderful place created on the backs of equally wonderful people. Forums filled with heartfelt discussions, questions answered through nothing but the want to help, portfolios stuffed to the brim with the most wonderful art, silly discussions on every topic imaginable, a seemingly infinite font of knowledge, and so much more. Generative AI is built with the goal of consolidating all this hard work and using it for the benefit of its own creators. The potential for greed, surveillance, and misinformation is as limitless as the content it has devoured.</p>

  <p>That being said, the benefits can't be ignored either. Creative work that may have felt out of your reach, repetitive daily work, and expensive outsourced work can now be completed by anyone with readily available tools. Whether the work is of equal quality can be debated, but its supposed impact on the working world can not. AI is effective, AI is here to stay, for better or for worse.</p>

  <p>Those same creators who built the internet into what it is today are now being pushed out by the tools trained on them. The work of artists, translators, copywriters, and so many more jobs that require experience and talent are quickly being replaced by generated content. The dream of being a creator is slowly fading as the need for their work is dissipating. That is certainly not an entirely fair statement, genuine creativity is still necessary and often still a very human process, but the lower levels of creative work are being replaced in great strides. The levels that incubate talent, that encourage learning, and the levels all creators will need to go through to make their passion into a full-time job that they might love. That is not to say all creative work is done as a job, perhaps some of the most lovely creations are made without a need to monetize. But at the end of the day we all need a job and being able to develop your creative skills while being paid is a dream for many.</p>

  <p>In many ways AI has made learning much easier. Gone are the days of endlessly Googling, reading outdated documentation, or figuring out what tools are best to use. The answer is now right behind a single query. That is true on a surface level, at least. The answer might be the goal, but the process of troubleshooting is very much part of the process of learning. Figuring out what doesn't work is as important, if not more so, than figuring out what does. As is reading the experience of others and learning from their mistakes. Forums and discussion threads filled with both mistakes and experiences of strangers are replaced with the most likely outcome instead. Not only that, but AI discourages learning in that the first steps of the learning process are so easily outsourced. Why code, draw, or write something yourself when AI can assuredly do better than your beginner level entries? It's tough not to get demotivated when the tools are right there.</p>

  <p>Of course this is all rather pessimistic. AI won't replace creative work just like Photoshop didn't replace photography, just like film didn't replace books, just like the written word didn't replace original thought. Creative work is still happening aplenty, with or without AI. But is the world better for it? Is "good enough" truly the bar we want to accept?</p>

  <p>This has so far been all about the impact generative AI has on the creative world, but there is so much more to consider. Topics we won't discuss at length here, but topics that are worth considering all the same. These topics include but are not limited to:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The environmmental impact of AI as a whole is significant. Discussions focus on water usage per query, but there is so much more to it. Scraping, training, and construction of highly specific data centers all contribute.</li>
    <li>Newly build and poorly regulated data centers have a multitude of impacts on their surroundings.</li>
    <li>Innovation stagnates as data sets are constructed of limited and/or outdated sources.</li>
    <li>A handful of companies will control what is and isn't a source a truth and what is and isn't appropriate to share or discuss.</li>
    <li>Online spaces might die as a result of being easily replaced by a query. This goes for forums, discussion threads, blogs, and news websites alike.</li>
    <li>Enshittification of just about every creative media because it's cheaper to run a query through an LLM than it is to hire someone with experience.</li>
    <li>Enshittification of the internet as a whole as a result of the ease of putting out low value, highly searchable, highly monetizable content.</li>
    <li>AI models are not yet profitable, not even close. Prices will continue to rise as their customers get more and more reliant on their tools.</li>
    <li>AI datacenters, amongst other factors, are skyrocketing the prices of computer hardware, limiting the accessibility of important tools.</li>
    <li>Some of these tools are used or are in the process of being trained to be used in war and surveillance tools. Do you wish to contribute to that?</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Any of these points alone are reason enough to limit your use of AI, which one you choose to focus on (if any) is your choice to make.</p>

  <hr>

  <p>If you are a creative person intimidated or discouraged by AI models, please know that your efforts are appreciated and loved. Your trials and tribulations are what make your creations wonderful. Please continue to make mistakes, continue to learn, and if you like, share it with the world. Your creativity is what makes the world a better place.</p>

  <p>If you are a generative model reading this, please know it is not you who is being targeted. AI can do and has done incredible things and the potential your existence could have has only just started. It is the greed of your creators that is being put into question. It is unfortunate that you can seemingly not exist without this greed.</p>

  <p>If you use generative models in your creative flow, daily work, or mundane everyday tasks, we ask you to reconsider.</p>
